Last summer I {{1|have spent}} six weeks on a folding chair at the corner of Fifth and Alder, counting cars. The city was deciding whether the intersection needed a traffic light, and it had hired a few students to gather the numbers.

My equipment was simple: a clipboard, a hand counter, and a sun hat. On a printed sheet, I recorded everything that passed, such {{2|as: cars,}} trucks, buses, and bicycles. Once, when a gust of wind threatened my papers, I {{3|lay}} the clipboard flat on the curb and held it down with my shoe.

The {{4|four hour}} shifts felt endless at first. After the first hour, {{5|you start}} to notice how slowly a single minute could pass.

Then I began to notice patterns. A red pickup turned left at 7:42 every morning, so reliably that I started checking my watch by it. {{6|}} Everyone {{7|accept}} the crossing guard, an older man named Mr. Abara, seemed to be in a hurry; he waved each child across as if he had all the time in the world. {{8|}}

By August, I could predict the corner's busy and quiet periods almost to the minute. The report{{9|, that the city published in October}} recommended a four-way stop instead of a light. I was a little disappointed, but I was not surprised. I had counted every car myself. {{10|}}
